<div id="outline-container-orgcfdd2a2" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="orgcfdd2a2">Mathematical environments</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-orgcfdd2a2">
<section class="math-env math-definition" id="orgba41768" aria-labelledby="orgba41768-heading">
<div class="math-heading" id="orgba41768-heading">Definition 1 <span class="math-name">— Upper bound</span></div>
<p>
Let \(A \subseteq \mathbb{R}\). A number \(M \in \mathbb{R}\) is an upper bound of \(A\) if \(x \leq M\) for every \(x \in A\).
</p>
</section>

<section class="math-env math-axiom" id="org75aed55" aria-labelledby="org75aed55-heading">
<div class="math-heading" id="org75aed55-heading">Axiom 2 <span class="math-name">— Completeness</span></div>
<p>
Every nonempty subset of \(\mathbb{R}\) that is bounded above has a supremum in \(\mathbb{R}\).
</p>
</section>

<section class="math-env math-lemma" id="orgb4542b9" aria-labelledby="orgb4542b9-heading">
<div class="math-heading" id="orgb4542b9-heading">Lemma 3 <span class="math-name">— Approximation from below</span></div>
<p>
If \(s = \sup A\), then for every \(\varepsilon > 0\) there exists \(a \in A\) such that
</p>

<p>
\[
s - \varepsilon < a \leq s.
\]
</p>
</section>

<section class="math-env math-theorem" id="orgfc0e3ab" aria-labelledby="orgfc0e3ab-heading">
<div class="math-heading" id="orgfc0e3ab-heading">Theorem 4 <span class="math-name">— Uniqueness of the supremum</span></div>
<p>
A nonempty subset of \(\mathbb{R}\) has at most one supremum.
</p>
</section>

<section class="math-env math-proof" id="org5afce32" aria-labelledby="org5afce32-heading">
<div class="math-heading" id="org5afce32-heading">Proof.</div>
<p>
Suppose \(s\) and \(t\) are both suprema of \(A\). Since \(t\) is an upper bound and \(s\) is the least upper bound, \(s \leq t\). Similarly, \(t \leq s\). Therefore \(s = t\).
</p>
<span class="math-qed" aria-label="End of proof">□</span>
</section>

<section class="math-env math-corollary" id="org6e86896" aria-labelledby="org6e86896-heading">
<div class="math-heading" id="org6e86896-heading">Corollary 5</div>
<p>
If \(A\) has a maximum \(m\), then \(\sup A = m\).
</p>
</section>

<section class="math-env math-examp" id="org9a418ad" aria-labelledby="org9a418ad-heading">
<div class="math-heading" id="org9a418ad-heading">Example 6 <span class="math-name">— A supremum without a maximum</span></div>
<p>
Let \(A = (0,1)\). Then \(\sup A = 1\), but \(A\) has no maximum because \(1 \notin A\).
</p>

<p>
For every \(x \in A\), the number \((x+1)/2\) also belongs to \(A\) and is strictly greater than \(x\).
</p>
</section>

</div>
</div>
